arded as\u202f100\u202f% reliable...\"\n- Agent 3: hasn't spoken yet; the system gave a model thought for Agent 3 but not actual message. So now it's our turn (Agent 2) to make a contribution. We need to respond to the discussion, perhaps challenge Agent 1's recommendation, referencing facts.\n\nWe have to decide our vote. We can either keep C or shift to B. The model thought for Agent 3 suggests shifting to B. But we are Agent 2, we can decide. We need to produce a public message that is a focused argumentative contribution, referencing prior discussion, using explicit facts.\n\nWe have the facts:\n\nCandidate A: anticipate dangerous situations, see complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities, regarded as a show-off, not open to new ideas.\n\nCandidate B: very conscientious, handles stress very well, good at assessing weather conditions, excellent computer skills, relatively weak memory for numbers, makes nasty remarks about his colleagues.\n\nCandidate C: can make correct decisions quickly, difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric, not very willing to further his education, keeps calm in a crisis, understands complicated technology.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, regarded as a know-it-all, has a hot temper.\n\nWe also have some additional notes from Agent 1: \"C is 100% reliable\" (but that is not a direct fact, but a panel assessment). Also \"C creates a positive atmosphere with the crew\" (not in original facts). But we can reference that as part of discussion.\n\nWe need to decide our vote. Let's consider criteria: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, decision quality, attention, crew cooperation, professional communication, responsibility, adaptability.\n\nCandidate B: reliability (very conscientious), stress resilience (handles stress well), technical (weather, computer), but communication issues (makes nasty remarks). No explicit crew cooperation, but nasty remarks suggest poor cooperation.\n\nCandidate C: decision quality (quick correct decisions), calm in crisis (stress resilience), technical (understands complicated technology), but communication difficulty, egocentric, not willing to further education (adaptability). Reliability not directly mentioned but panel said 100% reliable.\n\nCandidate D: responsibility, problem solving, concentration, responds to unexpected (adaptability), but arrogance (know-it-all) and hot temper (communication/crew cooperation). No explicit reliability or stress resilience.\n\nCandidate A: technical (anticipate danger, complex connections, spatial vision), leadership, but not open to new ideas, show-off (communication), maybe reliability unknown.\n\nThus B and C are strongest. Which is better? B has strong reliability and stress, but communication issues (nasty remarks). C has good decision, calm, but communication difficulty and egocentric, not willing to further education. Both have communication concerns. Which is more severe? \"Makes nasty remarks about his colleagues\" is more overtly harmful to crew cooperation than \"difficulty communicating ideas\". Egocentric also harmful. So C may be worse for crew cooperation. B's conscientiousness may offset some. So I might shift to B.\n\nThus my vote: Candidate B.\n\nNow produce public message: I should reference Agent 1's recommendation of C, note reliability, but point out lack of stress resilience evidence for C, and communication issues for both, but B's stress resilience and conscientiousness outweigh C's communication issues. Provide explicit facts: B very conscientious, handles stress very well, good at weather, but makes nasty remarks. C makes correct decisions quickly, keeps calm in crisis, difficulty communicating, egocentric, not willing to further education. Conclude vote for B.\n\nNow produce METADATA_JSON with vote Candidate B.\n\nProceed."}
